workedExample:
Ask what a thread *is*: an independent sequence of execution. To run independently it needs:

1. its own program counter (where it is in the code);
2. its own registers;
3. its own **stack**, because each thread is in a different chain of function calls with different local variables.

Everything else (code, heap, globals, open files) belongs to the process and is shared. So the stack is the per-thread item.

prompt:
Three threads each run `work()` once, concurrently. What does each thread print?

```c
int hits = 0;

void *work(void *arg) {
    int mine = 0;
    mine++;
    hits++;
    printf("mine=%d\n", mine);
    return NULL;
}
```
